# Kiosk hosted live demos — Caddy reverse proxy
#
# One vhost per demo subdomain -> a loopback Puma (systemd unit, see
# kiosk-demo@.service). Caddy handles automatic TLS (Let's Encrypt HTTP-01)
# per subdomain with zero cert plumbing.
#
# ── HOW THIS FILE REACHES THE BOX ───────────────────────────────────────────
# `deploy/deploy-caddy.sh` ships it whole: it stages the file, has the BOX's own
# caddy validate it, diffs, backs up, installs, reloads, then verifies the live
# wire and rolls back if the wire disagrees. Run `--check` to see the diff and
# change nothing. Do not hand-edit /etc/caddy/Caddyfile: this file is the source
# of truth and the script overwrites it entirely.
#
# THE NOTE THAT USED TO BE HERE SAID THE OPPOSITE, AND IT WAS FALSE. It warned
# that the live VPS is a SHARED host whose Caddyfile "also serves other sites",
# and that overwriting it "would drop the other sites' vhosts". MEASURED
# 2026-09-06 before the first deploy: /etc/caddy/ holds one Caddyfile and one
# old backup, nothing imports anything, and the RUNNING caddy's admin API lists
# exactly the eight demo hosts and no others. There were no other sites. The
# note cost a month: it is why the template was never applied, which is why the
# fleet sent no HSTS (K-1295) while this file shipped the header enabled, and
# why the fleet throttled every request (K-1168) while this file shipped the
# limiter commented out. A warning nobody can check is a warning nobody can
# retire -- so if this box ever does gain a non-Kiosk vhost, the answer is to
# make the script manage a fragment, not to write another note.
#
# Port map (must match env/<app>.env PORT and kiosk-demo@.service):
#   getgrocery -> 3001   atablefor -> 3002   hoteling  -> 3003
#   skooti     -> 3004   stylish   -> 3005   philslist -> 3006
#   tudu       -> 3007   kyc(prove)-> 3008
#
# ── TLS alternatives ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The per-subdomain blocks below get an individual HTTP-01 cert each — nothing
# extra to configure. If you prefer ONE cert for everything, use a wildcard
# *.demo.kiosk.tech cert via DNS-01 instead. That needs a DNS-provider plugin
# build of Caddy and an API token, e.g.:
#
#   *.demo.kiosk.tech {
#     tls {
#       dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}   # or your provider's module
#     }
#     @getgrocery host getgrocery.demo.kiosk.tech
#     handle @getgrocery { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001 }
#     ... one matcher per app ...
#   }
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Global options: contact address for Let's Encrypt expiry notices.
# Set a real ops mailbox before going live.
{
	# The ACME contact. This is what the live box has had since it was built, and
	# it is kept rather than "corrected" for a reason worth stating: changing it
	# makes Caddy register a NEW ACME account, which is a real action taken for no
	# benefit -- kiosk.tech publishes no MX (`dig +short MX kiosk.tech` is empty),
	# so neither this address nor any other on the domain receives anything today.
	# Whether the fleet should have a reachable operations address at all is its
	# own question and is not settled by a deploy script.
	email admin@kiosk.tech
}

# Reusable snippet: shared proxy + the per-IP edge rate-limit, which is OFF.
#
# ── THERE IS NO DEFAULT THROTTLE, AND THAT IS A DECISION (T-171) ─────────────
# Phil, 2026-09-05: «Наш PoW проверяется за миллисекунды. Ну, пускай сыплют
# мусором. Посмотрим, как реагировать. Делать по дефолту throttle - не надо.»
# The `import ratelimit` line and the (ratelimit) snippet below are commented
# out, this whole file is what the box runs (deploy/deploy-caddy.sh installs it
# entire), and the deploy's own wire check bursts an origin 75 times and fails
# if a 429 comes back. So the commented lines are the DEPLOYED posture, not an
# unfinished step.
#
# THEY ARE KEPT RATHER THAN DELETED. «Посмотрим, как реагировать» is removing
# the default, not forswearing protection: leaving the block here, commented,
# with the analysis beside it is what makes the next decision cheap.
#
# ── The exposure the limiter was for, which has not gone away (K-540) ────────
# `POST /kiosk/auth/register` runs the PoW gate UNAUTHENTICATED, ahead of any
# key verification: anyone can take a free 402 challenge and resubmit it with a
# valid HMAC sig but garbage indices. PoW prices the attacker's SOLVE; it never
# prices our VERIFY. And at the shipped WEB_CONCURRENCY=1 a plain flood of ANY
# endpoint — a 404, the 402 issue path itself — saturates the single worker just
# as well. The app can make each request cheaper; only something in FRONT of the
# app bounds the request RATE.
#
# ── Why the arithmetic changed ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app-side half of K-540 is shipped and measured (n=168 k=7, one box): one
# issued challenge drives at most one verify, and the verifier checks
# cheapest-first and hashes lazily, so a garbage proof costs 0.30 ms — or
# 0.012 ms if the attacker did not even order the indices — instead of the
# 18.7 ms it cost when every proof paid the full 128-hash loop. A real proof
# still costs ~18 ms. Worker saturation on this path moved from ~54 req/s to
# ~3.3k req/s. A proof verifies in 4.46 ms at the params this fleet runs, one
# worker completes a full registration 60 times a second and a plain read 1075
# times a second — and the limit that used to run on the box was ONE request a
# second, keyed per-IP across every vhost, so bursting one demo refused the
# other seven. That is a self-inflicted outage on the traffic these demos exist
# to receive, which is the other half of why it came off.
#
# ── If you do turn it back on ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `rate_limit` is NOT a stock Caddy directive — it comes from the third-party
# module github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit. A stock binary REFUSES to load a
# config naming it ("unrecognized directive: rate_limit" — verified on Caddy
# v2.11.2), so uncommenting it without the module means Caddy does not start and
# NO site serves at all. So:
#
#   1. Get a Caddy that HAS the module, e.g.
#        sudo caddy add-package github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit   # Caddy >= 2.7
#        sudo systemctl restart caddy
#      or build one:  xcaddy build --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit
#      Confirm:       caddy list-modules | grep rate_limit
#   2. Uncomment `import ratelimit` in (kioskproxy) AND the whole (ratelimit)
#      snippet below IN THIS FILE, then deploy/deploy-caddy.sh --apply. Do not
#      hand-edit /etc/caddy/Caddyfile: the next --apply would overwrite it, and
#      the deploy's wire check would then fail on the 429 it finds.
#   3. Pick the bound from a measurement, not from this file. The retired live
#      limit was 1 req/s and the snippet below says 60/min, and neither number
#      has a recorded derivation. Burst the origin and find where it degrades.
#
# ALTERNATIVE, equally acceptable: a per-IP rate rule on `/kiosk/*` at a CDN or
# WAF in front of this box. Nothing here can see that one, so if you take that
# route write it down where the next operator will read it — deploy/README.md
# under "Edge rate-limit".
#
# ── HSTS (K-916) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every vhost below imports this snippet, so the header is emitted once for the
# whole fleet rather than eight times by hand.
#
# WHY IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN THE RAILS APPS. `config.force_ssl` is the Rails
# way to get HSTS, and it is deliberately OFF in all eight apps: Caddy already
# terminates TLS and already redirects :80 -> :443, and the apps run behind it
# with `config.assume_ssl = true` (K-439), so turning force_ssl on would add a
# SECOND redirect for no gain. What force_ssl was ALSO doing, and what nothing
# then did, is emit `Strict-Transport-Security` — so until this directive the
# fleet had no HSTS at all, and a client typing a bare hostname made its FIRST
# request in plaintext, before the redirect. That first request is the window
# HSTS exists to close, and it is the only thing this line buys.
#
# `header` is a STOCK Caddy directive — unlike `rate_limit` above, it needs no
# module and therefore ships ENABLED rather than commented.
#
# max-age is one year, the value the preload list requires and the value a
# browser should be willing to remember. `includeSubDomains` covers a
# sub-subdomain nobody has created yet; there are none, and if one is ever
# added it must serve TLS. `preload` is deliberately ABSENT: submitting to the
# browser preload list is irreversible on any useful timescale, and these are
# demo hosts.
(kioskproxy) {
	encode zstd gzip
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	# import ratelimit          # OFF BY DECISION (T-171). See the note above before re-enabling.
	# Caddy forwards X-Forwarded-For/Proto/Host to the upstream by default.
	reverse_proxy {args[0]}
}

# (snippet) per-IP 60 req/min backstop — requires github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit.
# NOT DEPLOYED and not a pending step: see the T-171 note above. If you ever
# uncomment it, uncomment the `import ratelimit` line above with it, and treat
# the 60/min as a placeholder rather than a finding — it is far above any honest
# agent's pace (register once, then solve one PoW per tolled call — sub-second at
# the hosted `low`, ~10 s at `high`, on the M-series laptop core
# kiosk-pow-equihash/bench/README.md names) and far below what it takes to
# saturate a worker, but no measurement chose it.
# (ratelimit) {
# 	rate_limit {
# 		zone kiosk_per_ip {
# 			key    {remote_host}
# 			events 60
# 			window 1m
# 		}
# 	}
# }

getgrocery.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3001
}

atablefor.demo.kiosk.tech {
	# Flagship "book a table for two".
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3002
}

hoteling.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3003
}

skooti.demo.kiosk.tech {
	# "beware: intensive PoW" demo — high register difficulty (see env).
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3004
}

stylish.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3005
}

philslist.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3006
}

tudu.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3007
}

kyc.demo.kiosk.tech {
	# anonymizing KYC broker (kiosk-demo-prove). An ISSUER, not a Kiosk
	# operator — a human explainer + the /verify page + the operator intake/
	# callback JSON. No PoW, no /.well-known/kiosk.json, no agent surface.
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3008
}
